Actually, IP only DOES allow browsing in a sense.  It allows browsing 
     within each subnet (or workgroup when a workgroup spans a router) AND  
     allows browsing of NT servers in the domain.  This is perfectly 
     satisfactory on our net (for our users), although it would be nice for 
     us administrator types to be able to browse the entire net - but we 
     can work around that.
     
     It is true that enabling IPX on the entire net would allow browing of 
     the entire net - for the same reason that we want to get IPX off our 
     net - it would be bridged, not routed.  (I realise that it is possible 
     to route IPX, but I have no intention of messing with that.)
     
     ALSO, it is important to keep in mind when figuring out browsing, that 
     each protocol has its own browse list.  This can be quite confusing.  
     Only computers with Services loaded (MS or Netware) will been seen, 
     and they will be seen on separate lists even if the WG name is the 
     same.
     
     ALSO, in an IP only environment, use of WINS is essential, NetBios 
     computer names must be unique within the domain and should be the same 
     as IP host name (DHCP will make this true).  When WINS is properly set 
     up, no HOSTS or LMHOSTS files are required on any of the contemporary 
     machines (win95, NT3.5+, wfwg3.11) and WINS will pass unresolved name 
     requests to DNS (assuming you told WINS where the DNS servers are).
     
     Keep in mind that MS Networking is NetBios; period!  This is true no 
     mater what the transport protocol is.
     
     PLEASE CORRECT ME IF I LIE! jim

