From aaron@foo.garply.com Sat Jan 13 23:39:59 1996 CST
Message-ID: <v01530504ad1da7fbe1f7@[165.113.188.4]>
Date: Sat, 13 Jan 1996 12:39:19 -0600
From: Aaron Sakovich <aaron@foo.garply.com>
Subject: RE: WNT migration experiences and references ?

At 4:38 PM 1/12/96, Gilbert Armour wrote:
>I am curious as to why NETBUI is needed with IP?  We are moving from
>PATHWORKS 4.1 to NT using a mix of Intel and Alpha servers.  I know
>NETBUI is needed with DECNet protocol, but thought I could run pure IP.
>Windows supports NETBUI on IP (NBT), which leads me to believe it's not
>needed.  Multiple protocols can cause other problems.  I know broadcast
>messages go out on all loaded protocols, resulting in multiple messages.

I think you might be getting NetBIOS confused with NetBEUI.  NetBEUI is a
high performance, local area network transport.  NetBIOS is an API,
required for inter-system communications.

NBT is NetBIOS on TCP/IP, btw.  DECnet (a la PATHWORKS) utilizes NetBIOS
also (e.g., the "ncp define node m.nnn name xyzzy MS-NET" registers the
DECnet node as a NetBIOS server, allowing for access in a WAN which doesn't
support NetBIOS broadcast announcements -- quite similar to the INETNAME in
PATHWORKS, or the LMHOSTS file from MS.)

>Gilbert Armour

Aaron

