I returned my Millenium after finding the interrupt latency problem. I
believe there is a new beta NT driver that has a registry entry to fix it.
Some reports say it works. Matrox has not yet learned the quality level for
NT products will need to be MUCH better than it was for Win 3.x products.

I had waited to buy the Millenium for production NT drivers to ship. It only
me took two days of use to determine their definition of "production" didn't
cut it.

I found it so fast because I'm a devloper under NT since late '91 and know
when it's screwed up. I've  done device drivers for a LONG time and frown on
what Matrox is pulling. The Millenium card certainly breaks things. My stand
is Matrox is fudging things in their driver to get better performance. I
feel a bit like I'm being the NT driver police, but have a large personal
(both time and emotional) investment in NT. I'm not about to let a sloppy
hardware vendor hurt it's stability image. PC Magazine was quite interested
in what Matrox was doing and said they would have their technical people
investigate it. If they can only achieve top performance at the expense of
system stability (and breaking other things), they have a lot of explaining
to do. Personally, I'd rather have 10% less performance and a stable system,
which I currently have with a bland S3 based video card. I currently run a
#9GXE64 Pro. Quite fast and VERY stable with the S3 drivers included with NT.

The Millenium also only supports 16-bit z-buffers for it's 3D acceleration.
Many OpenGL apps on NT will not use the hardware accelerator if it only
supports 16-bit z-buffers. This makes the 3-D support of the Millenium
pretty useless.

The Millenium sounded attractive, but reality seems to be it's not. By the
time they get their driver act together, other, much better cards will
almost be here. I've decided to stick with good proven stuff now, and get
the new generation of stuff (3-D) sometime next year.

- Jan

