At 4:08 PM 1/24/96, Jonathan Stone wrote:
>Wow,
>
>So, are we saying that Microsoft and Digital ship two different versions
>of Windows NT that for the Alpha chip?

NO!

The CD is cut from the same master, only the silk-screening is different
(one says "Microsoft", the other, "Digital".)  Digital includes two extra
CD's plus an ECU floppy in their kit.  Microsoft includes 3 floppies
required by Intel systems to boot.

>-Jon Stone
>stonej@ug.eds.com
>
>> There is NOTHING signifigantly different about their version. I would
>> HIGHLY suggest getting the latest HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) files
>> from DEC and keeping with the MS version. We have seen some major
>> bulls&@% with 'Compaq's non-propriatary propriatary' version of NTS. Hear
>> me know and beleive me later, you do NOT want that headache - stick with
>> uniformity and standardization across your machines.

Compaq may have some proprietary problems, but not Digital.  The HAL code
is on the CD masters from MS -- heck, you could buy it from IBM and you
would get the Alpha HAL code.  What this person was possibly referring to
is the firmware for the ARC console and PAL code, that is shipped from
Digital on one of the "extra" CD's they include.  BTW, it's also available
from ftp.digital.com/private/fwaxp/.  The ECU can also be purchased
separately from Digital -- just call 800-DIGITAL and ask for the Alpha NT
ECU floppy, revision 1.8 or later.

Sincerely,
Aaron

