From jcrozier@mail.dos.state.fl.us Thu Jan 25 07:45:07 1996 CST
Message-ID: <9600258225.AA822588379@mail.dos.state.fl.us>
Date: Thu, 25 Jan 96 08:39:05 est
From: Jim Crozier <jcrozier@mail.dos.state.fl.us>
Subject: Re: User logon and home directory

Our primary configuration is Win95 clients to NT servers (Alpha and 
     Intel).  This conversion (from win3.11 and Netware) is only in the 
     early stages but we have established a plan).  There are a few 
     differences in the way Win95 clients are handled vs the way NT clients 
     are handled - and there is no documentation that I have found that 
     explains this (anyone who knows of some, plz let me know) - BUT we 
     have figured out how we intend to do it.
     
     I had a similar problem to the one described below and it had to do 
     with the fact that even though the NTFS directory permissions were 
     right, the permissions on the USERS share were wrong.  On problem 2 
     below, the only times that UMD has failed to create home directories 
     is when I've not done it correctly - but you will notice, that I avoid 
     this problem by creating the directory manually.
     
     Here are a few things we have decided to do to assure a uniform user 
     policy for our win95 users.  They do require a little more work when 
     the user is created but I believe that we will find that it will be 
     worth the effort.
     
     BTW, we have decided to create a separate hidden share for each user 
     and always map that to a consistant drive letter ("net use G: /home"). 
      This prevents a user from the chore of finding their own folder from 
     among all of the others in the USERS share.
     
     1) Create the user directory manually.
     2) Create a hidden share that references that directory.
     3) Create the user in UMD/
        a) under profiles:
                * the profiles path has meaning for NT clients only - leave 
     it blank for win95 clients
                * the login script named here will be run from the NETLOGON 
     share of whatever NT server validates the login - make sure your 
     replication is setup correctly.
                * press the "connect" radio button, the drive letter is 
     ignored by win95 client, and enter the unc for the home share you 
     created in step 2.
     4) Set the desired permissions on that user's home directory (we leave 
     the share permissions at the default - which is Everybody 
     FullControl).  Note that directory permissions are different from 
     share permissions.
     5) Set the user share on the client by the "net use /home" command in 
     the login script or by a permanent mapping in NetworkNeighborhood.
     
     This is the most satisfactory method we have found - there may be a 
     better one - I'm listening.  Note that the USERS share is visible and 
     available to any users that require (and have permissions) to other 
     directories there (such as shared work areas, etc).
     
     
     BTW, For those who have always used Netware Search drives:  The same 
     effect can be accomplished by using the "winset.exe" utility to append 
     specified drive paths to the PATH variable in the login script.  
     winset.exe is available on the web.

