Server (SMS) running on an alpha or intel box, which in turn requires =
Microsoft's SQL Server, running on the same (or different) alpha or =
intel box.

To use AssetWORKS, you define the packages you want to meter, and SMS =
distributes the instructions to the various desktops (AssetWORKS =
metering currently supports DOS, Windows, WFW, NT clients, and Win95 =
support has been announced for March). You can configure the time =
interval at which the clients report back, and view the data at the Site =
Server in a couple of different ways - using the PAW (Polycenter =
AssetWORKS) view (a GSW application that runs only on an Intel box) or =
the Access view (run-time MS Access engine supplied with AssetWORKS) =
that runs on either Intel or Alpha.  What you see is a graph of the =
number of concurrent users of the packages over time, and can configure =
alerts, etc.

Works well, and easy to configure (once you know SMS).  Entry price =
steep, however, since it is an add-on to SMS.  On the other hand, if you =
want to manage 30,000 desktops, SMS will save you $$$K per year of =
manpower...

