From: Brian Honey <brian.honey@citynet.net>
Subject: running newshell/no Task Manager workaround

I've read numerous posts concerning the Shell Technology Preview
where people have complained about losing the NT Task Manager, activated
by pressing CTRL+ESC. Under the NewShell, this key combination, of
course, brings up the Start Menu. Even though you can, by right-clicking
on the Taskbar, bring up the option to end a program, I miss the Task
Manager's clean functionality -- if a program goes bad, just hit
CTRL+ESC, click on the program name, and click End Task.

When running the NewShell, at least with the alpha version from the
FTP site, you can't even run the NT TASKMAN.EXE program. But, below
is a workaround I just discovered to this problem.

************************
Please Note: This *only* works if you're running a machine that can
             dual-boot Win95 and NT.
************************

I won't go into detailed instructions, but if you put a shortcut to the
*Win95* version of TASKMAN.EXE into your Start Menu Startup folder,
this TASKMAN.EXE will be available to you while running NT!

If you've never run the Win95 version of TASKMAN.EXE, it's pretty nice.
It's got pretty much all of the functionality of NT's TASKMAN.EXE, but
with different commands/options.

You can customize it so that just large, or small icons, or just text show,
and you can end programs (Windows/Close) and you can start programs as well
(File/Run). You can also set it Always On Top.

You can also assign a shortcut to it, so that you can bring it up with
a simple keyboard shortcut (mine is CTRL-ALT-Z), so you don't have to
ALT-TAB to it, or look at the NewShell Taskbar. I usually set the NT 
Taskbar
to AutoHide, so I just use TASKMAN.EXE for program/window management.

Sorry this post was so long, but I wanted to share this idea, just
stumbled on it today.

I wouldn't mind hearing from anyone who is lucky enough to be running
the NT NewShell Beta (I wasn't invited) if there is any kind of option
to use NT's TASKMAN.EXE in place of the Taskbar. . .

Brian

