Hello Adrian,

Regarding:
>>  On Jan 26 Philippe  (Subject: WNT 3.51 distribution kit)
>>  asked what ECU is. Eisa Configuration Utility is to set up
>>  the EISA-Bus Systems. Do you people with PCI Bus need that
>>  thing too? I wonder, 'cause I thought that all newer Alphas
>>  are equiped with PCI and ECU was mentionned a lot (never
>>  heard of PCU ...:)).

No, there's no need for a PCI configuration utility; either the RISC
firmware or the Intel NT loader configure the PCI bus.   The ECU is only
used to set up EISA options, not ISA; ISA options do not often have
enough on-board memory to hold configuration information.  The ECU will
manage information about ISA options if you have a configuration file
for them, but it doesn't write the configuration information into the
option, as it does with EISA options.  All this will become superfluous
when Plug 'N Play governs the design of ISA options.

>>  Anyway, I have a problem with my AXP 150: I installed a
>>  Sound Blaster Pro and also a Network Interface (Digital
>>  Turbo Etherwork or something) and they produce an IRQ
>>  conflict at start-up. I tried to tell the System to know the
>>  SB in Slot 3, but if I try to add in the ECU an ISA Board,
>>  it asks me for the System Configuration Utility. What the
>>  hell is that? I haven't found it on DECs Mailbox neighter by
>>  FTP. It's not to bad, the sound works (think that NT screws
>>  the IRQ by itself to #7), it's only because every time I
>>  boot it reminds me of that error and waits to continue till
>>  I type yes...

The default IRQ for the network interface is 5, which is the same as the
default IRQ for the Sound Blaster Pro.  You should probably use the
setup applet to reconfigure the network adapter to be IRQ 10, if that's
free.

>>  Thanx for any help...Adrian.

>>  PS: Someone asked about symbolic Unix-style Links, no
>>  answers??

Sorry, not from the user's viewpoint.  NT can do links, but they're not
exposed for use by applications.

Cheers,
James
