Hi,

Thanks a lot, Aaron, for sending me a copy of v 2.7.2 of the Gnu C compiler
promptly. I have got the gcc.exe as described below. But it did not work
in my system with 64RAM which complains something like in English " Programme
is too big to accommodate in memoryies". I hope it will work in other systems.

Nonetheless, as I promised I write how to transform the one Aaron gave into
the binary file. I tried it in unix boxes. I used SunSparc2 and the same
system I run winnt3.5 for alpha EB64+ in RedHat Linux 2.1 beta. You need
perl installed. Then ftp://utsun.cc.u-tokyo.ac.jp/fj/fj.sources/v55/5518.Z
which has tools to decode the original file. There are Japanese instructions
how to use the stuff. But never mind, even if you do not understand Japanese,
just delete lines you really do not understand upto begin ??? after you uncompress
5518.Z. By uudecode the resulting file, you obtain mime_pls-2.00alpha.tar.gz,
which should be decopressed in usual manner. You will get few files with Japanese
doc file as well that may be useless for some of you. Do not matter.
Then you have to cut Aaron's email file so that only unreadable codes remain,
i. e., cut everything from the top to a line "Content-Type: application/x-gzip"
and the following blank line, of course. Moreover cut the last line of
"---------------------------------22152471927583--" so that you will have now
a line starting, "H4sICAze+zAAAGdudW ..." and ending with a line
"iPpfc2Y91VM91VM91VM91VM91VM91VNt038BPkG/EwBYXAA=". I called the resulting file
gc. Then % perl rbody gc>gcc.gz. Now you have familiar file, gcc.gz
which can be decompressed by gzip -d gcc.gz. By renaming gcc to gcc.exe,
run it in NTalpha and see whether it will work. If it does, please let us know.

The above by no means is the best procedure but it is a workable one.

Cheers,

Akio Morita

